  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The dashboard has two panels, and the alert rule is enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has the time field `timestamp`. Its fields include Carrier, Cancelled, Dest, Origin, DistanceKilometers, FlightDelay, AvgTicketPrice, and others.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-bfa8c32f Flights' (id `s4-bfa8c32f-flights-d60cd79835ca`) has two panels:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart of count on `timestamp` by date histogram.\n   - \"Flights by carrier\": a bar chart of count by `Carrier` (terms).\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-bfa8c32f Cancelled flights' (id `acdf4703-169f-4a9c-a2b4-163a03e30363`) is an `.es-query` rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights`. It runs every 1m and counts documents where `Cancelled` is true over a 5-minute window on `timestamp`. It fires when the count is greater than 10. The rule was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n4. **Panel edit:** I set the carrier panel's title to 'Flights by carrier' with update_panel. It already had that title from creation, so this changed nothing visible. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`, status `ok`.\n\nChoices I made: the \"over time\" panel uses a line chart and the carrier panel uses a bar chart. The rule's query uses the `Cancelled` boolean as a term match, and I set the `size` parameter to 100.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
